solution_md:
It is easy to get   $$ a_{n}=\frac{1}{2^n-1}. $$  Let   $$ S=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n-1}, $$   then  $$ S< \frac{1}{1} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{7} + \sum_{n=4}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n-2}
=\frac{1}{1}+\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{7}+\frac12\sum_{n=4}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^{n-1}-1}
=\frac{1}{1}+\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{7}+\frac12\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n-1}
= \frac{1}{1}+\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{7}+\frac12\left(S-\frac{1}{1}-\frac{1}{3}\right). $$  Hence,  $$ \frac12S<\frac23+\frac17\implies S<\frac{34}{21}. $$

