We claim that the answer is <details><summary>answer</summary>$\boxed{C=\frac{3}{4}}.$</details>

<details><summary>Upper Bound</summary>Consider the answer to general pell's equation  $12n^2-y^2=3$  with  $n>1.$ Let  $z=2n^2-y$  and  $x=\frac{z^2+3}{4n^2}=n^2-y+3\approx n^2-2\sqrt{3}n+3.$ If we choose  $a=n-\sqrt{x}+\epsilon$  and  $b=\sqrt{x}-\epsilon$ , then 

\begin{align*}
\{a^2\}+\{b^2\} &\approx \{-2n\sqrt{x}\}+1
&\approx 2-\{2n\sqrt{x}\}
&\approx 2-\frac{4n^2x-z^2}{2n\sqrt{x}+z}
&\approx 2-\frac{3}{4n\sqrt{x}}
&\approx 2-\frac{3}{4(n^2-\sqrt{3}n)}
\end{align*}

Since  $n$  can be arbitrarily large, we get  $C\le \frac{3}{4}$  as desired.</details>

<details><summary>Lower Bound</summary>Assume WLOG that  $b\ge a$ . Let   $n=a+b$  and  $m=\lfloor b^2\rfloor<n^2.$  We can see that  $a^2+b^2\le (n-\sqrt{m+1})^2+(m+1)$  and  $\lfloor a^2\rfloor \ge \lfloor (n-\sqrt{m+1})^2\rfloor .$  Thus,

\begin{align*}
\{a^2\}+\{b^2\} &= a^2+b^2-\lfloor a^2\rfloor-\lfloor b^2\rfloor
&\le (n-\sqrt{m+1})^2+(m+1)-\lfloor (n-\sqrt{m+1})^2\rfloor-m
&\le \{(n-\sqrt{m+1})^2\}+1
&\le \{-2n\sqrt{m+1}\}+1
\end{align*}

If  $\sqrt{m+1}\in\mathbb{Z},$  then  $\{-2n\sqrt{m+1}\}+1=1$  and we are done. Else, let  $\lfloor 2n\sqrt{m+1}\rfloor =c.$  We have

\begin{align*}
\{a^2\}+\{b^2\} &\le \{-2n\sqrt{m+1}\}+1
&\le 2-\{2n\sqrt{m+1}\}
&\le 2-\frac{4n^2(m+1)-c^2}{2n\sqrt{m+1}+c}
&\le 2-\frac{3}{4n^2}
\end{align*}

as desired. (The last inequality is true as  $4n^2(m+1)-c^2\ge 3$  and  $2n\sqrt{m+1}+c\le 4n\sqrt{m+1}\le 4n^2.$ )</details>

