solution to P51( quite violent )
\subsection{Solution}
\textit{We can define  $y_i$  so that  $e^{y_i}$   $=$   $x_i$ . Since  $x_1x_2\cdots x_n=1$ , we have   $$ \sum_{x=1}^n y_i= 0 $$  }
\textit{then the problem comes to prove   $$ \sum_{x=1}^n e^{2y_i} + 9\sum_{x=1}^n e^{-y_i} \geq 10\sum_{x=1}^n e^{e^y_i} $$  }
\textit{   $$  \Longleftrightarrow \sum_{x=1}^n (e^{2y_i} + 9e^{-y_i} - 10e^{y_i}) \geq 0 $$  }
\textit{we define function  $f(x) = e^{2x} + 9e^{-x} - 10e^{x}$ }
\textit{\newline we know that  $f''(x) = 4e^{2x} - e^x + 9e^{-x}$ }
\textit{\newline by using AM-GM inequality, it's easy to that  $f''(x) > 0$ }
\textit{\newline As a result, by using Jensen inequality, we know    $$   \sum_{x=1}^n (e^{2y_i} + 9e^{-y_i} - 10e^{y_i}) \geq \sum_{x=1}^n f(\frac{\sum_{x=1}^n y_i}{n}) = 0 $$  }
\textit{ $QED.$ }

