Let  $Y(x)$  be the largest integer  $m$  so that there are  $m$  consecutive integers coprime to  $P_x$ . Now if we have  $$ Y(x) \gg \frac{x \log x}{(\log \log x)^2}, $$  then above result holds. The estimate of  $Y(x)$  is the key to the lower bound of **Large gaps between primes**.  Since we have  $$ G(x) := \max_{p_{n+1} \leqslant x} (p_{n+1} - p_{n}) \geqslant Y\left( (1+o(1)) \log x \right)  $$  as  $x \to \infty$ , by the above result we know that  $$ G(x) \gg \frac{\log x \log \log x}{(\log \log \log x)^2}. $$  This lower bound was first obtained by Erdős in 1935. The "furthermore" case   $$ G(x) \geqslant c (1+o(1)) \frac{\log x \log \log x \log \log \log \log x}{(\log \log \log x)^2}. $$  was first proved by Rankin in 1938 with  $c = \frac{1}{3}$ .
In 1963, Schönhage improved it to  $c = \frac{1}{2} e^{\gamma}$ ;
In the same year, Rankin increased it to  $c = e^{\gamma}$ ;
In 1990, Maier and Pomerance further improved it to  $c = 1.31256 e^{\gamma}$ ;
In 1997, Pintz got  $c = 2 e^{\gamma}$ .

In 2014 (published in 2016), Ford--Green--Konyagin--Tao (FGKT) and Maynard independently proved that the value of  $c$  in Rankin's result can be arbitrarily large. In the same year (published in 2018), FGKT--Maynard further improved this to  $$ G(x) \gg \frac{\log x \log \log x \log \log \log \log x}{\log \log \log x}. $$  This is the best result until now. A conjecture of Erdős states that we have  $$ G(x) > (\log x)^{1+\varepsilon} $$  with some  $\varepsilon > 0$ . Cramér's conjecture says:  $$ G(x) \sim (\log x)^2. $$

