<details><summary>Solution</summary>The answer is  $c=1$ . Let  $|V(G)|=n$  and construct a set  $S$  of vertices, with each vertex having probability  $p$  of being added to  $S$ . Then,  $\mathbb E[|S|]=pn$  and let  $U$  be the set of "undominated" vertices by  $S$ .\[\mathbb P(u\in U)=(1-p)^{\deg(u)+1}\le(1-p)^{N+1}\le e^{-p(N+1)}\] This gives  $\mathbb E[|U|]\le ne^{-p(N+1)}$ . Now construct a dominating set  $D$  such that  $S\subset D$  and keep adding a vertex at a time from  $U$  until it is dominating. This constructs a dominating set  $D$  with  $|D|\le |U|+|S|$ , and hence \[\mathbb E[|D|]\le \mathbb E[|U|+|S|]=\mathbb E[|U|]+\mathbb E[|S|]\le pn+e^{-p(N+1)}\] In particular, for  $p=\frac{\ln(N+1)}{N+1}$  we find a dominating set of size at most  $n\frac{\ln(N+1)+1}{N+1}$  which gives  $c\le 1$ .

For the lower bound, fix  $\varepsilon>0$  and  $n=N^{10^{10}+1}$ . Consider a random  $N+1$ -regular graph  $G$  on  $n$  vertices. Let  $\lambda=(1-\varepsilon)n\frac{\ln N}{N}$  (suppose this is an integer). Our goal is to show that with positive probability no set  $S$  of size  $\lambda$  dominates  $G$ , hence there will be some graph  $G$  which doesn't respect the condition. Fix a given set  $S$  of size  $k$ . Again, let  $U$  be the set of "undominated" vertices by  $S$ . Keep in mind that  $\frac{\lambda}{n}\to 0^+$ .

\begin{align*}
    \mathbb E[|U|]&=(n-\lambda)\mathbb P(u\in U)\sim (n-\lambda)\left(1-\frac{\lambda}{n}\right)^{N+1}
    &\ge(n-\lambda)\exp\left(-(N+1)\left(\frac{\lambda}{n}+\left(\frac{\lambda}{n}\right)^2\right)\right)
    &\ge (n-\lambda)N^{-(1-\varepsilon)\left(1+\frac{1}{N}\right)\left(1+\frac{\lambda}{n}\right)}\ge (n-\lambda)N^{-(1-\varepsilon)+o(1)}
    &\ge (1-o(1))nN^{-(1-\varepsilon)+o(1)}\ge (1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}
\end{align*}

Continuing, we compute the probability that the set  $S$  is dominating.
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb P(S\text{ is dominating})=\mathbb P(|U|=0)=(1-\mathbb P(u\in U))^{n-\lambda}&\le \exp(-\mathbb P(u\in U)(n-\lambda))
    &=\exp(-\mathbb E[|U|])
    &\le \exp\left((1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right)
\end{align*}

Finally, by union bound the probability that some  $S$  dominates is at most

\begin{align*}
    \binom{n}{\lambda}\mathbb P(|U|=0)&\le \left(\frac{en}{\lambda}\right)^{\lambda}\exp\left((1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right)
    &\le\exp\left(\lambda\ln\left(\frac{en}{\lambda}\right)-(1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right)
    &\le\exp\left(\lambda\ln\left(\frac{eN}{(1-\varepsilon)\ln N}\right)-(1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right)
    &\le\exp\left(c\frac{n\ln^2N}{N}-(1-o(1))N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right) \quad (\text{for a constant c that depends on } \varepsilon)
    &\le \exp\left(cN^{10^{10}}\ln^2N-(1-o(1)N^{10^{10}+\varepsilon+o(1)}\right)
\end{align*}

For sufficiently large  $N$ , the above quantity goes to  $0$ , hence the probability that no  $S$  dominates goes to  $1$ .  $\blacksquare$</details>

